## Upsert by a unique key

Create the unique index once (build-time semantics: REFERENCE.md, Index specs):

```json
{
  "request_type": "write",
  "query_name": "create_user_id_index",
  "query": {
    "write": {
      "entries": [
        {
          "query": {
            "name": "index",
            "root": {
              "create_index": {
                "spec": { "node_equality": { "label": "User", "property": "userId", "unique": true } },
                "if_not_exists": true
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "returns": ["index"]
    }
  }
}
```

The response is a receipt, e.g.
`{"index": {"kind": "accepted", "operation_id": "07070707-0707-0707-0707-070707070707", "index_id": "1", "generation": "1"}}`.
Poll until `status` is `succeeded`:

```json
{
  "request_type": "read",
  "query_name": "index_status",
  "query": {
    "read": {
      "entries": [
        {
          "query": {
            "name": "status",
            "root": { "get_index_operation": { "operation_id": "07070707-0707-0707-0707-070707070707" } }
          }
        }
      ],
      "returns": ["status"]
    }
  }
}
```

On `blocked`, fix the data its `blocker_code` names (for `uniqueness_violation`, the
existing duplicates) and send `{"retry_index_operation": {"operation_id": "..."}}` in a
write request. Then upsert in one atomic write batch:

```json
{
  "request_type": "write",
  "query_name": "upsert_user",
  "query": {
    "write": {
      "entries": [
        {
          "query": {
            "name": "existing",
            "root": {
              "nodes_where": {
                "predicate": {
                  "and": {
                    "predicates": [
                      { "eq": { "left": { "property": "$label" }, "right": { "constant": { "string": "User" } } } },
                      { "eq": { "left": { "property": "userId" }, "right": { "param": "user_id" } } }
                    ]
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "query": {
            "name": "updated",
            "condition": { "var_not_empty": "existing" },
            "root": {
              "set_property": {
                "input": { "nodes": { "reference": { "var": "existing" } } },
                "name": "name",
                "value": { "expr": { "param": "name" } }
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "query": {
            "name": "created",
            "condition": { "var_empty": "existing" },
            "root": {
              "add_n": {
                "label": "User",
                "properties": [
                  ["userId", { "expr": { "param": "user_id" } }],
                  ["name", { "expr": { "param": "name" } }]
                ]
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "returns": ["updated", "created"]
    }
  },
  "parameters": { "user_id": "u-1", "name": "Ada" }
}
```

One of `updated` / `created` is `[{"$id": N}]`, the other `[]`. A racing loser gets
`409 unique_constraint_violation` (`retryable: false`) or `409 transaction_conflict`
(`retryable: true`); re-run the upsert once and it takes the update branch.

## Optional neighbor (OPTIONAL MATCH)

`optional` passes the input row through when its branch is empty, so read the optional
side through a binding (a missing binding omits its key). `bind` and `project_bindings`
are read-only.

```json
{
  "request_type": "read",
  "query_name": "users_with_employer",
  "query": {
    "read": {
      "entries": [
        {
          "query": {
            "name": "rows",
            "root": {
              "project_bindings": {
                "input": {
                  "optional": {
                    "input": {
                      "bind": {
                        "input": {
                          "nodes_where": {
                            "predicate": { "eq": { "left": { "property": "$label" }, "right": { "constant": { "string": "User" } } } }
                          }
                        },
                        "name": "user"
                      }
                    },
                    "traversal": {
                      "root": {
                        "bind": {
                          "input": {
                            "has_label": {
                              "input": { "out": { "input": "context", "label": "WORKS_AT" } },
                              "label": "Company"
                            }
                          },
                          "name": "employer"
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  }
                },
                "projections": [
                  { "property": { "target": { "binding": "user" }, "source": "name", "alias": "name" } },
                  { "property": { "target": { "binding": "employer" }, "source": "name", "alias": "employer" } }
                ],
                "distinct": false
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "returns": ["rows"]
    }
  }
}
```

```json
{ "rows": [{ "name": "Ada", "employer": "Acme" }, { "name": "Bob" }] }
```

## Eventual search consistency

A read trading freshness of unpublished vector/text work for never failing with
`index_backpressure`:

```json
{
  "request_type": "read",
  "query_name": "similar_docs",
  "search_consistency": "eventual",
  "query": {
    "read": {
      "entries": [
        {
          "query": {
            "name": "hits",
            "root": {
              "value_map": {
                "input": {
                  "vector_search_nodes": {
                    "label": "Doc",
                    "property": "embedding",
                    "query_vector": { "expr": { "param": "vector" } },
                    "k": { "literal": 10 }
                  }
                },
                "properties": ["$id", "title", "$distance"]
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "returns": ["hits"]
    }
  },
  "parameters": { "vector": [0.1, 0.8, 0.2] }
}
```

## Cypher instead of the operation tree

A statement in the Cypher profile goes to `POST /v2/cypher`:

```json
{
  "query": "MATCH (u:User) OPTIONAL MATCH (u)-[:WORKS_AT]->(c:Company) RETURN u.name AS name, c.name AS employer ORDER BY name",
  "parameters": {},
  "query_name": "users_with_employer"
}
```

```json
{ "columns": ["name", "employer"], "rows": [["Ada", "Acme"], ["Bob", null]] }
```

## Invalid legacy shapes

Intentionally invalid (shown as `text` so JSON validation skips them):

```text
{"queries":[{"Query":{"steps":["Count"]}}],"returns":["count"]}
```

```text
{"request_type":"READ","query":{"read":{"entries":[],"returns":[]}}}
```

```text
{"request_type":"read","queries.json":{}}
```

For a shape not covered here, use an SDK serializer; the Rust, TypeScript, Python and Go
builders serialize to the same JSON.

## Interpret A Query Failure

Both of these are HTTP 409; only the first is worth resending:

```json
{ "error": "transaction_conflict", "msg": "<diagnostic>", "retryable": true }
```

```json
{ "error": "unique_constraint_violation", "msg": "<diagnostic>", "retryable": false }
```

`retryable: false` on a 409 means the database's state rejects the request: report the
duplicate or fix the state. Rules: SKILL.md, Failure envelope.

# Direct Query Examples

Canonical HelixDB v3 `POST /v2/query` requests. Cross-SDK contracts:
[source-canon.md](source-canon.md#contracts-every-query-skill-repeats).

## Core rules

- `request_type` is lowercase `read` or `write`.
- `query_name` is optional.
- `query` contains exactly one matching `read` or `write` batch.
- Each batch has ordered `entries` and `returns`.
- Operations form a nested `snake_case` tree; each chained operation wraps its
  predecessor under `input`.
- `parameters` and `parameter_types` are optional. When `parameter_types` is
  present, its keys exactly match `parameters`; typed and untyped values cannot
  be mixed in one request.
- One direct request per call; no stored routes or query bundles.
- Query warming is read-only.
- Node and edge IDs are JSON integers.
- A read may set `"search_consistency": "eventual"` (default `strong`).
- Cypher and SQL go to `POST /v2/cypher` / `POST /v2/sql` (and `/explain`) with
  `{"query", "parameters", "query_name"}`; use `/v2/query` for what their
  profiles reject (`MERGE`, variable-length paths, `UNION`, subqueries,
  `FOREACH`, search, index management).

## Minimal read request

```json
{
  "request_type": "read",
  "query_name": "node_count",
  "query": {
    "read": {
      "entries": [
        {
          "query": {
            "name": "node_count",
            "root": {
              "count": {
                "input": {
                  "nodes_where": {
                    "predicate": {
                      "eq": {
                        "left": { "property": "$label" },
                        "right": { "constant": { "string": "User" } }
                      }
                    }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "returns": ["node_count"]
    }
  }
}
```

## Parameters

Runtime values are ordinary JSON; add `parameter_types` when the SDK/query
declares them (the full request still needs the envelope and batch):

```json
{
  "parameters": {
    "created_after": "2026-04-05T10:00:00Z",
    "limit": 25
  },
  "parameter_types": {
    "created_after": "date_time",
    "limit": "i64"
  }
}
```

- Descriptors: `bool`, `i64`, `f32`, `f64`, `string`, `date_time`, `value`,
  `object`, recursive `array`. Raw bytes cannot be represented.
- Typed `f32`/`f64` accept integer and floating-point JSON numbers; values must
  be finite and `f32` within its finite range. Floats may be untyped, but a
  supplied `parameter_types` must describe every parameter.
- Body limit: 16 MiB locally (`400 invalid_request_body`), 2 MiB on Helix Cloud
  (`413 payload_too_large`); keep portable bodies ≤ 2 MiB. Nesting over 255
  levels is `400 invalid_query_json`.

## Write request

```json
{
  "request_type": "write",
  "query_name": "create_user",
  "query": {
    "write": {
      "entries": [
        {
          "query": {
            "name": "user",
            "root": {
              "add_n": {
                "label": "User",
                "properties": [
                  ["name", { "value": { "string": "Alice" } }]
                ]
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "returns": ["user"]
    }
  }
}
```

Response on a clean `ghcr.io/helixdb/helixdb:v0.0.16` instance (an unprojected
node is only its `$id`):

```json
{
  "user": [{ "$id": 0 }]
}
```

Row-stream returns are arrays (`[]` when empty); an empty `returns` list
produces `{}`. Writes wait for durability unless `X-Helix-Await-Durable: false`
is sent (see source-canon for both contracts).

## Query warming

Send the same read body with `X-Helix-Warm: true` (or `1`; `false`/`0` is the
ordinary path).

- Local server: executes the read and returns `200 OK` with the normal response.
- Helix Cloud: the gateway fans the read out to every eligible backend, discards
  the responses and returns `204 No Content` once one target succeeds (partial
  failure still `204`; all failing returns the normal error).
  `X-Helix-Require-Writer: true` warms only the writer. No eligible target is
  `503 Service Unavailable`.
- Any other header value, or a warm write, is `400` (`invalid_request_option`
  locally) before execution. Auth, read rate limits, retries and query timeouts
  still apply.
- The Cloud `204`: TypeScript 3.2.0 accepts it (`send()` resolves `undefined`);
  published Rust 3.0.0, Python 0.3.4 and Go 0.3.1 accept only 200 and surface
  it as a remote error (SDK `main` accepts it), so warm Cloud over direct HTTP
  from those.
- The CLI's `--warm` flag is local-only; it cannot warm a Cloud database.

## Query Failures

Non-success responses, including through the Cloud gateway, are
`{"error":"<code>","msg":"<diagnostic>","retryable":true|false}`. Both
`transaction_conflict` (`retryable: true`) and `unique_constraint_violation`
(`retryable: false`) are HTTP 409: branch on `error`/`retryable`, never on the
status or `msg`. No `code` field; keep unknown codes as open strings. Classes,
actions and Cloud gateway codes: [error-handling.md](error-handling.md).

## Common mistakes

Do not:

- put a route-name string under `query`
- send a query-bundle file
- use `mcp` as `request_type`
- use the obsolete `queries` plus `steps` AST
- use PascalCase enum tags
- mismatch `request_type` and the batch variant
- reverse the `error` and `msg` meanings, require a `code` field, or retry
  every 409
- send node or edge IDs as strings
- document internal `current`/`bindings` rows as the response

